# Tunables for llama-server-full-128k.service - primary daily-driver config.
#
# Installed by install.sh to ~/.config/llama-server/full-128k.env and passed
# to llm-server.sh via --tunables-file by systemd/llama-server-full-128k.service.
# Edit values here and `systemctl --user restart llama-server-full-128k.service`
# to pick them up - no need to touch the .service file or llm-server.sh.
#
# These happen to match llm-server.sh's own built-in defaults already, since
# "full-128k" is the fastest single-GPU-only setup - but they're listed
# explicitly here so this file is a complete, self-contained record of the
# deployment, and so this stays true even if the script's defaults change.
# Anything left commented uses llm-server.sh's shared default for that
# value. Uncomment and edit any line to override it. Full explanations of
# each value: ../scripts/llm-server.sh --help

# --- values that define this deployment (uncommented for visibility) ---
LLAMA_NGL=99
LLAMA_NCMOE=27
LLAMA_THREADS=12
LLAMA_CTX_SIZE=131072

# --- memory stability (uncommented - see README's "Memory stability" section) ---
# ctx-checkpoints is at llama-server's own default (32). It was previously
# set to 0 here on the belief that checkpoints never restore on this
# architecture - that was WRONG and cost real performance. Measured on
# llama.cpp b10087: checkpoints are what lets the prompt cache below
# actually restore a branch. With them at 0, returning to a cached branch
# reprocessed the whole prompt (11-14k tokens, 13-16s); with them enabled,
# the same switch cost 25 tokens and 0.3s. See README's "Memory stability"
# section for the full measurement.
#
# 32 is not a reservation: checkpoints are allocated on demand, and 4, 8 and
# 32 all measured identical in both benefit and memory on a 2-branch
# workload. The number is only a ceiling on how many divergence points a
# busy session may keep.
#
# cache-ram is back at llama-server's own 8192 MiB default (not cut down)
# after measuring the actual working set this repo's sequential-subagent
# workflow needs: ~11 KiB/token (only ~10-11 of 41 layers are full
# attention, per the GGUF's full_attention_interval=4 - the rest are
# fixed-size recurrent state), so a max-256k-context branch costs ~2.75GiB
# and 8192 MiB covers ~3 of them resident at once (orchestrator + active
# subagent + title/summary + slack). Cutting this to "save" desktop RAM
# previously turned fast orchestrator<->subagent handoffs into 90+ second
# full reprocesses - see README's Memory stability section before lowering
# it again.
#
# The branch list above is not just agents. A coding client also issues
# background requests that aren't agents at all, and each is a distinct
# prefix competing for this same pool:
#   - title generation, fired concurrently with the first message of every
#     session (observed on the wire: the title request started ~11s before
#     the main one completed, and waited behind it on the single slot)
#   - auto-summarization as a session nears its context limit
# Routing those to a non-thinking preset cuts their generation time but not
# the slot contention or the cache branch - same weights, same one slot.
# 8192 may well already cover them; the point is that whoever tunes this
# next should know those branches exist.
#
# VALIDATED 2026-07-25: it does cover them. At the measured ~36 KiB/token
# per cache entry, a *full* 131072 branch is only ~4.6 GiB, so 8192 holds one
# max-context branch plus a second of ~97k tokens. Measured end-to-end:
# returning to a 125,701-token branch after seeding a 28,909-token second
# branch cost reproc=5 / 0.24s. Unlike full-256k, this deployment can always
# cache a full-width branch - do not lower this to "save" RAM (it is a cap,
# not a reservation: resting footprint was identical at 8192 and 10240).
LLAMA_CACHE_RAM=8192
LLAMA_CTX_CHECKPOINTS=32

# --- everything else (commented = using llm-server.sh's shared default) ---
#LLAMA_MODEL_PATH="$HOME/llama-server/models/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MTP-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf"
#LLAMA_PARALLEL=1                # must stay 1 while LLAMA_SPEC_TYPE=draft-mtp
#LLAMA_CACHE_TYPE_K=q8_0
#LLAMA_CACHE_TYPE_V=q8_0
#LLAMA_SPEC_TYPE=draft-mtp
#LLAMA_SPEC_DRAFT_N_MAX=4        # tuned by testing - falls off sharply above 4
# Sampling below is a per-request DEFAULT, not deployment policy: any API
# client sending its own sampler block overrides it, and coding agents send
# one on every call (OpenCode sends temperature/top_p/top_k/presence_penalty
# /min_p/repeat_penalty each time). These reach raw curl and silent clients
# only - tune an agent's sampling in that agent's config. Verify with /slots,
# not /props (/props shows only the server's CLI-baked baseline).
#LLAMA_TEMP=1.0
#LLAMA_TOP_P=0.95
#LLAMA_TOP_K=20
#LLAMA_PRESENCE_PENALTY=1.5
#LLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0
#LLAMA_PORT=8080
#LLAMA_NO_MMAP=1                 # 1 = pass --no-mmap, 0 = allow mmap
#LLAMA_FLASH_ATTN=on
#LLAMA_EXTRA_ARGS=""
